-- Scene 4: what is REFUSED, and why --
  [ok]   text that is no XML: refused -- MusicXML: not well-formed XML -- there is no element at all
  [ok]   XML that is not well-formed: refused -- MusicXML: not well-formed XML -- </score-partwise> closes <part>
  [ok]   score-timewise: refused -- MusicXML: score-timewise is not read; only score-partwise, the form nearly every program writes
  [ok]   a compressed .mxl: refused -- MusicXML: this is a compressed .mxl (a zip); unzip it and read the .musicxml inside
  [ok]   an XML that is not a score: refused -- MusicXML: the root is <html>, not <score-partwise>; this is not a MusicXML score
  [ok]   a duration before any divisions: refused -- MusicXML: a note has a duration before any <divisions> says what a quarter note is

-- What MU9 does NOT do --
   score-timewise and compressed .mxl are refused. Jumps (D.C., D.S., coda) are not
   followed; glissandos are read as their first pitch; one tempo per score. The key
   signature is not needed: MusicXML writes every alteration on its note.

